Accession Number   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  Report Status  Updated Type  Surgical Pathology Pathology Report  MS 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  CASE  MS 10  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME  Date Taken  REMOVED_DATE Source Care Unit  Same Day Surgery  WACC 3 Path Subspecialty Service  Breast 1 Results To  MICHELLE C SPECHT  M D  Resident  Evan Farkash  M D   Ph D  Signed Out by  Melinda J Lerwill  M D             Addended Report             Reason for Addendum  1  Additional Immunohistochemistry Results CLINICAL DATA  Ductal carcinoma in situ on core biopsy  Mass and calcifications  FINAL DIAGNOSIS  BREAST  RIGHT   EXCISION  1  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU WITH CALCIFICATIONS  SEE NOTE  1 AND TABLE  1  2  FOCAL INTRALYMPHATIC TUMOR  SEE NOTE  1  3  FLAT EPITHELIAL ATYPIA AND ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  4  FOCAL FIBROCYSTIC CHANGES WITH CALCIFICATIONS  5  SMALL MYXOID FIBROADENOMA  6  FOCAL LACTATIONAL CHANGES  NOTE  1  The ductal carcinoma in situ demonstrates confluent involvement of a 2 0cm area and forms a mass lesion  Definite evidence of stromal invasion is not identified  A few well preserved  compact tumor clusters are seen within lymphatic vessels in the main excision  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and the separate inferior margin specimen  block  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   see below   The intralymphatic tumor may represent displaced carcinoma  but the possibility of an occult invasive carcinoma cannot be entirely excluded  Epithelial displacement can occur after core biopsy or needle localization wire placement  Immunohistochemical stains for calponin  smooth muscle myosin heavy chain  p63  and cytokeratin were examined on blocks  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID  and support an interpretation of confluent in situ carcinoma  The specimen has been entirely submitted for histologic examination  The localized calcifications are associated with benign changes  TABLE OF PATHOLOGICAL FINDINGS  1 DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU TUMOR SIZE  The ductal carcinoma in situ forms a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm mass lesion  GRADE  2  CALCIFICATIONS  In the carcinoma in situ and in benign changes  MARGIN OF DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  Focally present at and broadly  0 1cm from the inferior margin  ductal carcinoma in situ also extends to 0 1cm from the anterior margin in one focus  STAINS FOR RECEPTORS  Will be attempted on the limited amount of intralymphatic tumor  See also  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   BREAST  RIGHT  INFERIOR MARGIN   EXCISION  1  DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU  SPANNING ABOUT REMOVED_DATE CM AND PRESENT  0 2 CM FROM THE NEW MARGIN  2  FOCAL INTRALYMPHATIC TUMOR  SEE NOTE  1 ABOVE  3  ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA  4  HEALING BIOPSY SITE  NOTE  2  Immunostains for  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   and p63 were examined  BREAST  RIGHT  POSTERIOR MARGIN   EXCISION  THERE IS NO EVIDENCE OF MALIGNANCY  BREAST  RIGHT  LATERAL MARGIN   EXCISION  FOCAL FLAT EPITHELIAL ATYPIA  NOTE  The resection margins of the specimens were inked and microscopically evaluated  GROSS DESCRIPTION  Received fresh in a single container   labeled REMOVE_PATIENT_NAME   REMOVE_MRN and  right breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 3 1cm portion of fibroadipose tissue with 2 needle localization wires in place  which has been oriented by the surgeon with a short stitch designating the superior aspect and a long stitch designating the lateral aspect  The long axis runs medial to lateral  The specimen is inked as follows  Anterior   blue  posterior   black  superior   green  inferior   yellow  The specimen is serially sectioned perpendicular to the long axis to reveal a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 8cm lobulated  firm  white mass adjacent to needle localization wire  2  which is located 0 9cm from the medial margin  2 4cm from the lateral margin  1 7cm from the posterior margin  1 8cm from the superior margin  0 1cm from the anterior margin and abutting the inferior margin  There is an area of slight grittiness in the superolateral portion of the specimen in the region adjacent to the needle localization wire  1  The remainder of the parenchyma is composed of unremarkable yellow adipose tissue and white fibrous tissue  The entire specimen is submitted as follows   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Medial end  en face   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Mass  entirely submitted from medial to lateral   REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Lateral aspect of specimen  from medial to lateral  containing potential calcifications  with lateral tip in  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID    REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Remainder of specimen  Received are an additional 3 specimens  all labeled REMOVE_MRN  Received in formalin  labeled  inferior margin  right breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 3cm portion of yellow adipose and white fibrous tissue  which has been oriented by the surgeon with a stitch designating the new margin  The new margin is inked black  and the specimen is serially sectioned and entirely submitted as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Received in formalin  labeled  posterior margin  right breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 1 2cm portion of yellow adipose tissue  which has been oriented by the surgeon with a stitch designating the new margin  The new margin is inked black  and the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal no lesions or masses  and it is entirely submitted as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Received in formalin  labeled  lateral margin  right breast   is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x 0 9cm portion of yellow adipose tissue and white fibrous tissue  which is oriented by the surgeon with a stitch designating the new margin  The new margin is inked black  and the specimen is serially sectioned to reveal dense  white fibrotic tissue abutting the new margin  The specimen is entirely submitted as  REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID   Final Diagnosis by Melinda J Lerwill M D   Electronically signed on Sunday REMOVED_MONTH 24   REMOVED_CASE_ID  at 05 07 53PM By his her signature above  the pat